  <regions type="content">
    <region left="36" top="18" width="646" height="24" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 10px; font-family: DejaVu Sans;">Industrial Air Compressor Capacity Estimation via Receiver Pump-Up Test Dynamics</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="63" width="749" height="258" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Test Procedure:</p>
          <p>  • The receiver tank outlet isolation valve is tightly closed.</p>
          <p>  • The compressor charges the empty receiver vessel (V = 2.50 m³) from 0.0 barg to 6.0 barg.</p>
          <p>  • The plant DCS historian records high-resolution pressure P(t) and temperature T(t) telemetry.</p>
          <p>  • The compressor's instantaneous and average throughput is back-calculated via numerical calculus.</p>
          <p>The Isothermal Overestimation Trap:</p>
          <p>Plant engineers may assume the vessel remains at constant temperature (isothermal assumption) for simplicity in calculations, however, as hot air enters from the aftercooler, vessel's internal temperature rises. Gas thermal expansion inflates the apparent pressure rise (dP/dt). Neglecting this thermal effect overestimates compressor capacity by 5% to 15%, <span style="font-weight: bold; font-family: DejaVu Sans;">masking degraded machine performance!</span></p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="333" width="271" height="24" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="DejaVu Sans" fontSize="10">
